import type { QuestionSeed } from "./types";

/** Further Hard Questions. Original FCGBS text; where Christians differ, the views are stated fairly; history and archaeology are limited to what is well documented. */
export const MORE_QUESTIONS: QuestionSeed[] = [
  { slug: "who-was-melchizedek", category: "Origins and the Bible",
    title: "Who was Melchizedek?",
    summary: "Melchizedek was a real king-priest of Salem who blessed Abraham (Genesis 14). Psalm 110 makes his 'order' the pattern of the Messiah's priesthood, and Hebrews 7 shows that Christ is a priest like him, not by descent but by an indestructible life. Views differ on whether he was an ordinary Canaanite ruler who worshipped the true God, or an appearance of the Son before He became man; the New Testament itself says only that he was 'made like unto the Son of God'.",
    scriptures: ["Genesis 14:17-24", "Psalms 110:1-7", "Psalms 76:1-3", "Hebrews 5:5-10", "Hebrews 6:19-20", "Hebrews 7:1-28"],
    readings: ["Paul Ellingworth, The Epistle to the Hebrews (NIGTC)", "William Lane, Hebrews 1–8 (Word Biblical Commentary)", "Gordon Wenham, Genesis 1–15 (WBC)", "Fred Horton, The Melchizedek Tradition"],
    exegetical: `## The Genesis account
After Abraham's victory over the eastern kings, "Melchizedek king of Salem brought forth bread and wine: and he was the priest of the most high God" (Genesis 14:18, KJV). He blesses Abraham in the name of "the most high God, possessor of heaven and earth" (14:19), and Abraham gives him a tenth of the spoils (14:20). He appears suddenly and disappears; the text says nothing of his parents, birth or death.

## Salem and the name
*Melek-tsedek* (H4442) means "king of righteousness" (or "my king is righteousness"). Salem (*Shalem*, H8004, from *shalom*) is identified with Jerusalem in Psalm 76:2, "In Salem also is his tabernacle, and his dwelling place in Zion" (KJV).

## Psalm 110
David, writing of the Messiah, says: "The LORD hath sworn, and will not repent, Thou art a priest for ever after the order of Melchizedek" (Psalm 110:4, KJV). A king who is also a priest is something the law of Moses did not allow (kings from Judah, priests from Levi). Psalm 110 therefore points to a new kind of priest-king.

## Hebrews 5–7
The writer explains the verse. Melchizedek's name and city show that he is "first being by interpretation King of righteousness, and after that also King of Salem, which is, King of peace" (Hebrews 7:2, KJV). The silence of Genesis about his ancestry is read as a picture: he is "Without father, without mother, without descent, having neither beginning of days, nor end of life; but made like unto the Son of God; abideth a priest continually" (Hebrews 7:3, KJV). Because Abraham (and so Levi, in Abraham's loins) paid tithes to him and received his blessing, his priesthood is greater than the Levitical one (7:4–10). Since perfection did not come through the Levitical priesthood, another priest was needed "after the order of Melchisedec" (7:11), and Jesus is that priest "after the power of an endless life" (7:16), appointed by God's oath (7:20–22).

## Was Melchizedek a man or the Son?
1. **A historical king who served the true God.** Most commentators read Genesis 14 as describing a Canaanite city-king who, like Job, knew the one true God. Hebrews says he is "made like" the Son (*aphomoiomenos*, G871), which means a likeness, not an identity. The writer works from what Genesis does not say.
2. **A pre-incarnate appearance of Christ (a theophany).** Some early and later interpreters (and some modern evangelicals) hold that he was the Son in human form. The argument: no beginning or end of days, and priest for ever. Against it: Hebrews compares him to the Son and calls Christ "another priest" who "ariseth after the similitude of Melchisedec" (7:15), which is awkward if they are the same person.
3. **An angel or exalted being.** The Qumran text 11Q13 (below) and some Jewish writings picture a heavenly Melchizedek. The Bible does not teach this.`,
    archaeological: `- **Jerusalem before David.** The Amarna letters (Egypt, about 1350 BC) include letters from Abdi-Heba, ruler of *Urusalim*, a Canaanite city-king who calls himself appointed by the king of Egypt, not by his father or mother. This shows that Jerusalem was an important city-state with a local ruler in the Late Bronze period, a setting consistent with a king of Salem. (Abraham's own date is earlier and debated.)
- **King-priests in the ancient Near East.** Kings often served as chief priests (Egyptian pharaohs, Ugaritic and Mesopotamian kings), so a king who is "priest of the most high God" fits the culture; what is distinctive is the worship of one God, *El Elyon*. A Ugaritic god-name *El* and the title "Most High" (*Elyon*) are attested in Canaan and in an Aramaic treaty from Sefire (8th century BC).
- **11QMelchizedek (Qumran).** A scroll from the first century BC presents Melchizedek as a heavenly figure who proclaims release at the end of the age, quoting Psalm 82 and Isaiah 61. It shows that Melchizedek was a subject of expectation in Judaism when Hebrews was written. It is not Scripture.`,
    apostolic: `Hebrews is the apostolic reading: Christ's priesthood does not depend on bloodline or law but on "the power of an endless life" (Hebrews 7:16). Because He "continueth ever, hath an unchangeable priesthood" (7:24), He is "able also to save them to the uttermost that come unto God by him, seeing he ever liveth to make intercession for them" (7:25, KJV). The bread and wine of Melchizedek have been seen by many Christians as a foreshadowing of the Lord's Supper, though Hebrews does not make that point itself.`,
    prophetic: `Psalm 110 is the most-quoted Old Testament chapter in the New Testament. Jesus used its first verse to silence His questioners (Matthew 22:41–46), and Peter used it at Pentecost (Acts 2:34–35). The two verses together, "Sit thou at my right hand" and "a priest for ever", present the Messiah as both king and priest. Zechariah 6:12–13 also speaks of a "Branch" who will "be a priest upon his throne".`,
    theological: `## What matters
1. **Christ is both King and Priest,** the only mediator (1 Timothy 2:5). No one else needs to stand between a believer and God.
2. **The Levitical system was a shadow** that is now fulfilled (Hebrews 8–10).
3. **God's revelation is wider than Israel.** Melchizedek, a non-Israelite, served the true God; grace and faith were never restricted by bloodline.
4. **Do not build on speculation.** The New Testament refers only to what Genesis says and to Psalm 110. Details of who he "really" was belong to curiosity, not doctrine.`,
    views: `Most evangelical and Reformed commentators treat him as a historical priest-king who foreshadows Christ. Some Church Fathers (and a minority today) take him as a Christophany. Some Jewish tradition identifies him with Shem, Noah's son, which has no support in the text. Everyone who reads Hebrews agrees that Christ's priesthood is superior and permanent.`,
    pastoral: `The comfort of Melchizedek is that our High Priest lives for ever and intercedes for us. Invite believers to approach God confidently (Hebrews 4:16), without any other priest or intermediary, and to remember that their prayers are carried by a priest who never dies.` },

  { slug: "divorce-and-remarriage", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "What does the Bible teach about divorce and remarriage?",
    summary: "God's design is a lifelong covenant between one man and one woman, and He hates the violence and faithlessness that break it. The Bible nonetheless recognises grounds on which a marriage may be ended (sexual immorality, and desertion by an unbelieving partner), and Christians differ on how far these extend and on whether remarriage is permitted. The church must uphold marriage firmly while treating the divorced, and the abused, with compassion.",
    scriptures: ["Genesis 2:18-24", "Deuteronomy 24:1-4", "Malachi 2:13-16", "Matthew 5:31-32", "Matthew 19:3-12", "Mark 10:2-12", "Romans 7:1-3", "1 Corinthians 7:10-16", "1 Corinthians 7:39-40", "Hosea 3:1-3"],
    readings: ["David Instone-Brewer, Divorce and Remarriage in the Church", "William Heth and Gordon Wenham, Jesus and Divorce", "Craig Keener, And Marries Another", "Wayne Grudem, Systematic Theology (chapter on marriage and divorce)", "Divorce and Remarriage: Four Christian Views (IVP)"],
    exegetical: `## God's intention
"Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh" (Genesis 2:24, KJV). Jesus quotes this and concludes: "What therefore God hath joined together, let not man put asunder" (Matthew 19:6, KJV).

## The Old Testament law
Deuteronomy 24:1–4 does not command divorce; it regulates an existing practice, requiring a written certificate when a man divorces his wife for "some uncleanness" (*ervat davar*) and forbidding him to remarry her after she has been married to another. Malachi 2:16 says: "For the LORD, the God of Israel, saith that he hateth putting away: for one covereth violence with his garment" (KJV). God's anger is directed at treacherous divorce that wrongs the wife.

## Jesus
Asked by the Pharisees whether it is lawful to divorce "for every cause" (Matthew 19:3), Jesus goes back to creation. When they cite Moses, He says, "Moses because of the hardness of your hearts suffered you to put away your wives: but from the beginning it was not so" (Matthew 19:8, KJV). He adds: "And I say unto you, Whosoever shall put away his wife, except it be for fornication, and shall marry another, committeth adultery" (Matthew 19:9, KJV). Mark 10:11–12 and Luke 16:18 record the saying without the exception, in the context of the lifelong claim of marriage.
The "exception" is *porneia* (G4202), a broad word for sexual immorality. Whether it refers only to adultery, to any serious sexual sin, or to something particular (such as unchastity found during betrothal) is debated.

## Paul
- To the married: "Let not the wife depart from her husband" (1 Corinthians 7:10), and if she does, "let her remain unmarried, or be reconciled to her husband" (7:11).
- To believers married to unbelievers: "But if the unbelieving depart, let him depart. A brother or a sister is not under bondage in such cases: but God hath called us to peace" (1 Corinthians 7:15, KJV). Many read "not under bondage" as freedom from the marriage and, so, to remarry.
- Romans 7:1–3 illustrates that death ends the marriage bond; the widow "is free from that law".

## How Christians understand it
1. **No divorce and remarriage while the partner lives** (a strict reading of Mark 10 and Luke 16). Held by the Catholic Church (which allows separation and annulment of invalid marriages) and by some Protestants.
2. **Divorce for adultery, and remarriage for the innocent party** (the Reformation and Westminster Confession view), often extended to desertion.
3. **Divorce for adultery and desertion, and remarriage permitted** to those who were divorced on biblical grounds, with some including abuse as a form of covenant-breaking (Instone-Brewer's reading of Exodus 21:10–11).
4. **A pastoral approach:** marriage is for life, divorce is a tragic failure, yet the grace of God covers repentant sinners, and remarriage is not an unforgivable offence.`,
    archaeological: `- **Jewish divorce in Jesus' time.** The school of Shammai restricted divorce to sexual unfaithfulness; the school of Hillel allowed divorce for almost any displeasure (Mishnah, *Gittin* 9:10). The Pharisees' question in Matthew 19:3 ("for every cause") is the Hillel–Shammai debate.
- **Divorce documents.** A Hebrew and Aramaic divorce certificate from Wadi Murabba'at (about AD 72) uses language close to Deuteronomy 24: "you are free to go and marry any Jewish man you wish". Elephantine marriage contracts (5th century BC) show that women there could initiate divorce, unlike in most Jewish law.
- **Roman law.** Either party could end a Roman marriage easily, and divorce was common in the Roman upper classes. This is the setting in which Paul addresses the Corinthians.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles upheld marriage: "Marriage is honourable in all, and the bed undefiled: but whoremongers and adulterers God will judge" (Hebrews 13:4, KJV). Paul teaches that husbands should love their wives "even as Christ also loved the church" (Ephesians 5:25). They also provided pastoral realism: "God hath called us to peace" (1 Corinthians 7:15).`,
    prophetic: `The prophets use marriage as a picture of God's covenant. Hosea is told to take back an unfaithful wife (Hosea 3:1–3), showing God's persistent love. Yet Jeremiah 3:8 says that God also "gave her a bill of divorce" to unfaithful Israel, showing that unrepentant faithlessness has consequences. The final marriage is between Christ and His bride (Revelation 19:7).`,
    theological: `## Principles
1. **Marriage is a covenant, not a contract,** made before God, meant for life.
2. **Divorce is never God's ideal,** and Jesus says it was allowed because of hardness of heart.
3. **God's grace is greater than failure.** The divorced and remarried are not second-class Christians, and many have a faithful walk with God.
4. **Abuse is serious.** A spouse is not required to stay in danger; safety comes first. Many churches teach that abuse and sustained cruelty are a breach of the marriage covenant, and that separation is appropriate. The church must protect the vulnerable.
5. **Church leaders and marriage:** churches differ on whether a divorced man may serve as an elder ("the husband of one wife", 1 Timothy 3:2).`,
    views: `The Roman Catholic Church holds marriage to be indissoluble and offers annulment in cases where a valid marriage never existed. Orthodox churches allow divorce and remarriage in certain circumstances, with penitential rites. Protestant churches vary widely: Reformed and Baptist churches generally accept divorce for adultery and desertion, some also for abuse. All urge reconciliation first.`,
    pastoral: `For couples in trouble: pray, seek counsel and wise mediation early. For those who have been divorced: listen without condemnation and remind them of God's forgiveness (1 John 1:9). For abuse victims: believe them and help them to safety. For leaders: avoid a quick answer. Study the passages with the church board and decide a consistent, compassionate policy.` },

  { slug: "baptism-mode-meaning-and-infants", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "Baptism: what does it mean, how should it be done, and what about infants?",
    summary: "All Christians baptise because Jesus commanded it, in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit, as the sign of cleansing, union with Christ's death and resurrection, and entry into the church. They differ on the mode (immersion, pouring or sprinkling), on whether it is for believers only or also for infants of believing parents, and on how much it does (a sign, or a means of grace). Faithful believers differ here, and unity does not depend on agreeing.",
    scriptures: ["Matthew 3:13-17", "Matthew 28:18-20", "Acts 2:37-41", "Acts 8:34-39", "Acts 10:44-48", "Acts 16:29-34", "Romans 6:1-11", "1 Corinthians 1:14-17", "Colossians 2:11-13", "1 Peter 3:20-21", "Galatians 3:26-29"],
    readings: ["George Beasley-Murray, Baptism in the New Testament", "Anthony Lane and others (eds.), Baptism: Three Views (IVP)", "Thomas Schreiner and Shawn Wright (eds.), Believer's Baptism", "Joachim Jeremias, Infant Baptism in the First Four Centuries"],
    exegetical: `## The command and the name
"Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost" (Matthew 28:19, KJV). In Acts, believers are baptised "in the name of Jesus Christ" (Acts 2:38; 10:48), which names the one into whom they are baptised; it does not contradict the Trinitarian formula.

## The meaning
- **Cleansing and forgiveness:** "Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins, and ye shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost" (Acts 2:38, KJV). The phrase "for the remission of sins" is debated: some read baptism as a means by which forgiveness is received, others as the sign that accompanies repentance and faith.
- **Union with Christ:** "Therefore we are buried with him by baptism into death: that like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life" (Romans 6:4, KJV). Baptism pictures dying and rising with Christ.
- **Entry into the body:** "For by one Spirit are we all baptized into one body" (1 Corinthians 12:13). Galatians 3:27: "as many of you as have been baptized into Christ have put on Christ."
- **A pledge:** 1 Peter 3:21 calls baptism "the answer of a good conscience toward God", saved "by the resurrection of Jesus Christ".

## The mode
The Greek *baptizo* (G907) means to dip, immerse or wash. Immersion is the plain sense and is suggested by "they went down both into the water" (Acts 8:38) and the language of burial (Romans 6:4; Colossians 2:12). Pouring or sprinkling is defended from Old Testament cleansing rites (Ezekiel 36:25), from the pouring out of the Spirit (Acts 2:17) and from situations where large amounts of water were not available (the Philippian jailer, Acts 16:33, baptised at night in prison).

## Who may be baptised
- **Believers only (credobaptism).** Every New Testament example is of those who heard, repented and believed (Acts 2:41; 8:12; 18:8). Baptism follows faith.
- **Believers and their children (paedobaptism).** Households were baptised (Acts 16:15, 33; 1 Corinthians 1:16), and Colossians 2:11–12 links baptism to circumcision, which was given to infants. Peter says that "the promise is unto you, and to your children" (Acts 2:39, KJV). The argument is for continuity of the covenant.
- Objection: no infant is explicitly mentioned, and the "household" accounts mention faith and joy of the whole household (Acts 16:34).`,
    archaeological: `- **Mikva'ot.** Dozens of stepped, plastered immersion pools have been excavated in Jerusalem (especially near the Temple Mount's southern steps), Qumran, Masada and Galilean towns. Immersion was a regular part of Jewish purity practice, so John's baptism and early Christian baptism had a recognisable setting. It also explains how thousands could be baptised in Jerusalem on one day (Acts 2:41).
- **The Didache** (about AD 100) instructs: baptise "in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit in running water. But if you do not have running water, baptise in other water; and if you cannot in cold, then in warm. But if you have neither, pour water on the head three times." It shows both immersion as the norm and pouring as acceptable in the second generation after the apostles.
- **Dura-Europos.** A house church (about AD 240–256) in Syria has a room with a baptismal font and wall paintings of the Good Shepherd and the healed paralytic, the oldest known Christian building adapted for worship.
- **Infant baptism.** Tertullian (about AD 200) argued against baptising little children, which shows that it was practised; Origen (about AD 240) says the church received the tradition from the apostles. Early catacomb epitaphs show the baptism of young children by the third century.`,
    apostolic: `Paul says, "For Christ sent me not to baptize, but to preach the gospel" (1 Corinthians 1:17, KJV), not to belittle baptism but to protest against dividing the church around who baptised whom. He holds to "One Lord, one faith, one baptism" (Ephesians 4:5, KJV). Peter links the water of the flood with baptism (1 Peter 3:20–21). The apostolic pattern is repentance, faith, baptism and the gift of the Spirit.`,
    prophetic: `Ezekiel 36:25–27 promises: "Then will I sprinkle clean water upon you, and ye shall be clean... A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you" (cf. Ezekiel 36:25–26). John the Baptist announced that the One coming would baptise "with the Holy Ghost and with fire" (Matthew 3:11). Water baptism looks to this inner washing.`,
    theological: `## Main traditions
- **Baptist, Pentecostal and many evangelical churches:** believers only, by immersion, as a sign and obedience (an "ordinance"). It does not itself convey salvation.
- **Reformed, Presbyterian and Anglican churches:** infants of believers and adult converts, by sprinkling or pouring (or immersion), as a sign and seal of the covenant.
- **Lutheran, Catholic and Orthodox churches:** baptism is a means of grace in which God works new birth and forgives sin (Orthodox baptise by triple immersion, including infants).
- **Churches of Christ and some others:** baptism is necessary for the forgiveness of sins.
- **Quakers and Salvation Army:** do not practise water baptism, holding that Spirit baptism is what matters.

## What all can say
1. Baptism was commanded by Christ, and every believer should be baptised.
2. It is not a mere ritual: it is a public confession of faith and union with Christ.
3. Salvation is by grace through faith in Christ, not by water as such.
4. Christians should not despise or rebaptise lightly those whose baptism was sincerely performed in a different tradition, and should not make this issue a cause of division.`,
    views: `The debate is old (Tertullian, Augustine, the Anabaptists and the Reformers, Baptists and Presbyterians). In the world today the majority of Christians are baptised as infants (Catholic, Orthodox, Lutheran, Anglican), while Pentecostal and Baptist churches are among the fastest growing. Learn from one another.`,
    pastoral: `Encourage new believers to be baptised soon, with testimony. For parents, explain your church's practice kindly and respect other convictions. For those baptised in infancy who later come to personal faith, seek counsel from your pastor and be gentle with those who see it differently.` },

  { slug: "why-so-many-denominations", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "Why are there so many denominations? Is Christian unity possible?",
    summary: "Christ prayed that His people would be one, yet the church is divided into Catholic, Orthodox, Protestant and thousands of independent bodies. Some divisions came from truth that mattered (the Reformation), some from language, politics and personalities, and some from sin. The Bible calls for unity in the essentials, liberty in matters on which faithful Christians differ, and love in everything.",
    scriptures: ["John 17:20-23", "Romans 14:1-23", "1 Corinthians 1:10-17", "1 Corinthians 3:1-9", "Ephesians 4:1-6", "Ephesians 4:11-16", "Galatians 1:6-9", "Philippians 1:15-18", "Acts 15:1-35", "Jude 1:3"],
    readings: ["Kevin Vanhoozer, Biblical Authority after Babel", "Mark Noll, Turning Points: Decisive Moments in the History of Christianity", "J. I. Packer and Thomas Oden, One Faith: The Evangelical Consensus", "Timothy Tennent, Invitation to World Missions", "Ephraim Radner, A Brutal Unity"],
    exegetical: `## Christ's prayer
"That they all may be one; as thou, Father, art in me, and I in thee, that they also may be one in us: that the world may believe that thou hast sent me" (John 17:21, KJV). Unity is rooted in the unity of the Father and Son and is a witness to the world.

## Division in the New Testament church
The apostolic church was never free of disagreement. In Corinth there were slogans: "Now this I say, that every one of you saith, I am of Paul; and I of Apollos; and I of Cephas; and I of Christ. Is Christ divided?" (1 Corinthians 1:12–13, KJV). Paul and Barnabas had "a sharp contention" over John Mark (Acts 15:39). Jewish and Gentile Christians argued over the law (Acts 15). In Rome the issue was food and holy days (Romans 14).

## Two kinds of unity commanded
1. **Unity in the faith:** "One Lord, one faith, one baptism, One God and Father of all" (Ephesians 4:5–6, KJV). The apostle Paul curses anyone who preaches a different gospel (Galatians 1:8–9). Jude urges believers to "earnestly contend for the faith which was once delivered unto the saints" (Jude 3). So some divisions are required when the gospel is at stake.
2. **Unity in love and in disputable matters:** "Him that is weak in the faith receive ye, but not to doubtful disputations" (Romans 14:1, KJV). "Endeavouring to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace" (Ephesians 4:3, KJV). On matters where sincere believers differ, the command is to receive, not to despise or judge.

## The principle
A well-known summary attributed to various writers (Augustine, Rupert Meldenius and Richard Baxter) is: "In essentials, unity; in non-essentials, liberty; in all things, charity." The difficulty is deciding what is essential. Historic creeds (the Apostles' and Nicene Creeds) are the widely accepted statement of the essentials: the Trinity, the deity and humanity of Christ, His death and resurrection, salvation by grace and His return.`,
    archaeological: `A short history of the divisions shows their causes:
- **Councils and creeds.** The councils of Nicaea (AD 325), Constantinople (381), Ephesus (431) and Chalcedon (451) defined the doctrines of the Trinity and Christ. Some churches that rejected Ephesus or Chalcedon (the Assyrian Church of the East; the Oriental Orthodox churches of Egypt, Ethiopia, Armenia and Syria) have survived to this day; recent dialogues find that many of the differences were partly questions of language.
- **East and West (1054).** The mutual excommunications of Rome and Constantinople reflected centuries of differences in language, culture, authority of the pope and the *filioque* clause in the creed.
- **The Reformation (from 1517).** Luther, Zwingli, Calvin and the Anabaptists broke with Rome over authority, justification by faith and the sacraments, and then differed from one another (the Lord's Supper, baptism, church government).
- **Later movements.** Methodism (18th century), the Baptist movement (17th century), Pentecostalism (from 1906, Azusa Street), and African Initiated Churches (from the late 19th century) arose from revivals, missionary work and the desire for indigenous leadership.
- **Archaeology** adds the early evidence of a church that, despite disagreements, shared baptism, the Lord's Supper, Scripture reading and the confession of Jesus as Lord, as seen in the house church at Dura-Europos and in the catacombs of Rome.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles worked for both truth and unity. At Jerusalem, "it seemed good to the Holy Ghost, and to us" to settle the Gentile question without division (Acts 15:28). Paul collected an offering from Gentile churches for Jerusalem to show their solidarity (Romans 15:25–27). He rejoiced when Christ was preached even from wrong motives (Philippians 1:15–18), but opposed those who distorted the gospel itself (Galatians 2).`,
    prophetic: `Ezekiel 37:15–23 foresees two sticks becoming one in God's hand: a reunited people. Jesus speaks of "one fold, and one shepherd" (John 10:16). The hope of the redeemed in Revelation 7:9 is a great multitude "of all nations, and kindreds, and people, and tongues". The final unity is God's gift.`,
    theological: `## Which divisions are sinful?
- **Divisions from pride, personality, race, tribe, money or power are sinful.** "For whereas there is among you envying, and strife, and divisions, are ye not carnal?" (1 Corinthians 3:3, KJV).
- **Separation for the sake of the gospel is sometimes required,** when essential truth (the deity of Christ, salvation by grace) is denied.
- **Differences of tradition are not necessarily division:** the church is one body with many members (1 Corinthians 12).

## What believers can do
1. Know and confess the historic essentials together.
2. Pray with and work with other churches in the community, in mission, relief, Bible translation and evangelism.
3. Avoid caricatures of other traditions; learn what they actually teach.
4. Keep your own convictions with humility; avoid the language of contempt.
5. Remember that the church is bigger than your congregation or denomination, and that the Lord knows His own (2 Timothy 2:19).`,
    views: `Catholics and Orthodox hold that visible unity requires a common communion and apostolic succession; Protestants emphasise the invisible unity of all believers and cooperation across denominations; the ecumenical movement (World Council of Churches), the Lausanne Movement and local church alliances (such as Christian Associations in many nations) are different efforts. Pentecostals and charismatics have strong cross-denominational links.`,
    pastoral: `Be a peacemaker in your community. Join in prayer with other churches, support local pastors' fellowships, and teach your congregation to treat other Christians as brothers and sisters. Warn against "church shopping" driven by consumerism and against the pride that thinks one's group alone is right.` },

  { slug: "spirits-in-prison-1-peter-3-and-christs-descent", category: "Jesus Christ",
    title: "Did Jesus descend into hell? What are the 'spirits in prison' (1 Peter 3:19)?",
    summary: "The Apostles' Creed says Christ 'descended into hell', but the Bible's few related texts are debated. 1 Peter 3:19 (spirits in prison) is read as Christ preaching through Noah, as Christ proclaiming victory to fallen angels, or as Christ preaching to the dead. Scripture does not teach a second chance after death or purgatory on this basis. What is clear is that Christ truly died, was in the realm of the dead, and rose triumphant.",
    scriptures: ["Psalms 16:8-11", "Luke 23:39-43", "Acts 2:22-32", "Ephesians 4:7-10", "1 Peter 3:18-22", "1 Peter 4:1-6", "Genesis 6:1-8", "Jude 1:5-7", "Revelation 1:17-18"],
    readings: ["Wayne Grudem, Systematic Theology (chapter on the descent)", "Karen Jobes, 1 Peter (BECNT)", "Wayne Grudem, 'He Did Not Descend into Hell' (JETS 34, 1991)", "Thomas Schreiner, 1, 2 Peter, Jude (NAC)", "Dennis Johnson, 1 Peter (ESV Expository Commentary)"],
    exegetical: `## The key text
"For Christ also hath once suffered for sins, the just for the unjust, that he might bring us to God, being put to death in the flesh, but quickened by the Spirit: By which also he went and preached unto the spirits in prison; Which sometime were disobedient, when once the longsuffering of God waited in the days of Noah, while the ark was a preparing" (1 Peter 3:18–20, KJV). Verse 22 says Christ is "gone into heaven, and is on the right hand of God; angels and authorities and powers being made subject unto him".

## Interpretations
1. **Christ preaching through Noah.** By His Spirit, the pre-incarnate Christ preached through Noah to the people of Noah's day, who are now "spirits in prison" because they rejected the message. (Augustine, many Reformers.) Support: the context speaks of Noah's time and of Christ's Spirit speaking through the prophets (1 Peter 1:11).
2. **Christ proclaiming victory to fallen angels.** After His resurrection (or at His ascension) Christ announced His triumph to the rebellious angelic powers imprisoned since Genesis 6 (compare Jude 6; 2 Peter 2:4). The word "preached" (*kerusso*, G2784) means to announce or proclaim, not necessarily the gospel offered for salvation. Support: "spirits" in the New Testament normally means angelic beings; the Jewish tradition of 1 Enoch describes imprisoned fallen angels from Noah's day; verse 22 mentions subjected powers. Many modern commentators hold this view.
3. **Christ preaching the gospel to the dead between His death and resurrection** to give them an opportunity to repent. (Clement of Alexandria; some modern Christians.) Support: 1 Peter 4:6, "For for this cause was the gospel preached also to them that are dead". Objections: Scripture says that after death comes judgement (Hebrews 9:27); 1 Peter 4:6 may refer to those who heard the gospel when alive and have since died.

## "He descended"
The Apostles' Creed (in its later form, about the fourth century) says Christ "descended into hell" (Latin *ad inferos*, "to the realms below", meaning Hades or the grave). Related texts: Psalm 16:10 (applied in Acts 2:27, "thou wilt not leave my soul in hell, neither wilt thou suffer thine Holy One to see corruption"); Ephesians 4:9 ("he also descended first into the lower parts of the earth"), which may mean His descent to earth in the incarnation or to the grave; and Christ's word to the thief, "To day shalt thou be with me in paradise" (Luke 23:43, KJV). Interpretations of the creed vary: that Christ truly died and entered the state of the dead (Reformed, Anglican), that He suffered in hell (Calvin's reading of Christ's agony), or that He entered Hades to free the righteous dead (the Eastern "harrowing of hell").`,
    archaeological: `- **1 Enoch.** Aramaic fragments of the Book of the Watchers (1 Enoch 6–16) from Qumran (4Q201–202, third to second century BC) describe the angels who sinned in Genesis 6 being bound in prison until the judgement, and Enoch proclaiming a message to them. This is the likely background for Peter's readers, who would have recognised the idea of imprisoned spirits from the days of Noah. It does not make 1 Enoch Scripture.
- **The tradition of the descent in early art and liturgy.** From the fourth century, the "Harrowing of Hell" (Christ breaking the gates of Hades and raising Adam) appears in the apocryphal Gospel of Nicodemus (fourth to fifth century) and in Byzantine icons of the resurrection (the *Anastasis*). They illustrate later reflection, not apostolic teaching.
- **The creed's history.** The phrase "descended into hell" is absent from the earliest forms of the Roman creed and is first found in a creed of Aquileia (about AD 390).`,
    apostolic: `Peter's purpose is pastoral: believers who suffer should remember that Christ suffered, was vindicated and rules over all powers. He ends with the triumph: Christ is at God's right hand with "angels and authorities and powers being made subject unto him" (1 Peter 3:22). Paul writes similarly that Christ "spoiled principalities and powers, he made a shew of them openly, triumphing over them" (Colossians 2:15).`,
    prophetic: `Isaiah 24:21–22 foretells that "the LORD shall punish the host of the high ones that are on high" and that they "shall be gathered together, as prisoners are gathered in the pit, and shall be shut up in the prison". Psalm 68:18 (cited in Ephesians 4:8) says the Lord "led captivity captive". The victory over the powers of darkness was foretold, and the New Testament sees it in Christ's death, resurrection and ascension.`,
    theological: `## What we can affirm
1. **Christ truly died.** His soul and body were separated; He entered the state of the dead (Acts 2:27, 31).
2. **He rose bodily, triumphant over death, Hades and the powers.** "I am he that liveth, and was dead; and, behold, I am alive for evermore, Amen; and have the keys of hell and of death" (Revelation 1:18, KJV).
3. **There is no biblical basis for purgatory or a second chance after death** built on 1 Peter 3. Hebrews 9:27 says that "it is appointed unto men once to die, but after this the judgment".
4. **The gospel is for the living now.** "Behold, now is the accepted time; behold, now is the day of salvation" (2 Corinthians 6:2, KJV).
5. **Humility.** This is one of the most difficult passages in the New Testament (Augustine said he did not know what it meant). Hold your reading with care.`,
    views: `Reformed and Baptist churches usually take view 1 or 2 and the creed's "descended" as referring to Christ's death and burial; Lutherans see a victorious descent after the resurrection; Orthodox and Catholic traditions speak of Christ freeing the righteous dead; some liberal Protestants and a few evangelicals suggest a post-mortem opportunity, which most reject.`,
    pastoral: `When recited in the creed, explain that "descended into hell" means that Christ fully entered death for us. Comfort the grieving with the promise that Christ has the keys of death. Avoid speculation about a second chance; instead urge people to respond to the gospel now.` },

  { slug: "blasphemy-against-the-holy-spirit", category: "Salvation and the Christian life",
    title: "What is the unforgivable sin (blasphemy against the Holy Spirit)?",
    summary: "Jesus said that every sin and blasphemy can be forgiven except blasphemy against the Holy Spirit. In context, it was said to teachers who saw Jesus' work done by the Spirit and called it demonic. It is a settled, hardened rejection of the Spirit's testimony to Christ, not a slip of the tongue, a doubt or a sin a believer fears he has committed. A person troubled that they may have committed it shows by that concern that they have not.",
    scriptures: ["Matthew 12:22-32", "Mark 3:20-30", "Luke 12:8-12", "Hebrews 6:4-8", "Hebrews 10:26-31", "1 John 5:16-17", "1 John 1:9", "Isaiah 1:18", "John 6:37", "Romans 8:1"],
    readings: ["D. A. Carson, Matthew (Expositor's Bible Commentary)", "R. T. France, The Gospel of Matthew (NICNT)", "Craig Evans, Mark 8:27–16:20 (WBC)", "John Stott, The Message of 1 John"],
    exegetical: `## The context
Jesus heals a demon-possessed man who was blind and mute. The people wonder if He is the Son of David, but the Pharisees say, "This fellow doth not cast out devils, but by Beelzebub the prince of the devils" (Matthew 12:24, KJV). Jesus replies with a logic argument (a kingdom divided cannot stand) and then says: "Wherefore I say unto you, All manner of sin and blasphemy shall be forgiven unto men: but the blasphemy against the Holy Ghost shall not be forgiven unto men" (Matthew 12:31, KJV). Mark explains why: "Because they said, He hath an unclean spirit" (Mark 3:30, KJV).

## What it is
The Pharisees saw clear evidence of God's power in Jesus' works and deliberately attributed the Spirit's work to Satan. The sin is:
- **Blasphemy** (*blasphemia*, G988): speaking against, slander, directed at **the Holy Spirit**, who bears witness to Christ.
- **Persistent and deliberate:** a hardened rejection of the clearest light, calling good evil (Isaiah 5:20).
- **Unforgivable** not because God's mercy has limits, but because the person who rejects the only means of forgiveness (the Spirit's testimony to Christ) will not repent or ask for it.

## The difference between speaking against the Son and the Spirit
Jesus also says, "whosoever speaketh a word against the Son of man, it shall be forgiven him" (Matthew 12:32). Many who opposed Jesus in ignorance were later forgiven (Paul, a former "blasphemer", 1 Timothy 1:13; Peter who denied Him). The unforgivable sin is the final, informed and settled rejection of the Spirit's witness.

## What it is not
- **It is not doubt, fear or a moment of anger** against God. Job, Jeremiah and many psalmists spoke boldly to God and were not condemned.
- **It is not a sin a believer commits.** Believers who are troubled by sin are being convicted by the Spirit, which shows He is at work in them.
- **It is not "any" sin.** The Bible says, "If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness" (1 John 1:9, KJV).
- **It is not the "sin unto death"** of 1 John 5:16, which is a separate and debated matter (probably persistent apostasy from the faith).

## Related warnings
Hebrews 6:4–6 and 10:26–29 warn of those who, having known the truth, "do despite unto the Spirit of grace" (Hebrews 10:29). The same pastoral question arises: these are descriptions of deliberate and final apostasy, not of struggling believers.`,
    archaeological: `Jewish teachers of Jesus' day also believed in exorcisms and expected the Messiah to heal. Josephus (*Antiquities* 8.45–49) describes an exorcist named Eleazar who drove out a demon in Vespasian's presence, and the Qumran *Genesis Apocryphon* has Abraham praying for the healing of Pharaoh by exorcism. The Gospels record that rival exorcists existed (Matthew 12:27), and that Jesus' opponents accepted that He did powerful signs. The Jewish charge of sorcery against Jesus recurs in later rabbinic literature. The accusation that He worked by Beelzebul (*Baal-zebul*, "lord of the house", from a Canaanite title mocked in 2 Kings 1:2 as "Baal-zebub") shows that the opposition could not deny the miracles, only their source.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles invite sinners to repent: "Repent ye therefore, and be converted, that your sins may be blotted out" (Acts 3:19, KJV). Paul, who persecuted the church and blasphemed, "obtained mercy, because I did it ignorantly in unbelief" (1 Timothy 1:13, KJV). He calls himself the chief of sinners to show that the worst may be forgiven. John says that "the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin" (1 John 1:7, KJV).`,
    prophetic: `Isaiah 63:10 says Israel "rebelled, and vexed his holy Spirit". Stephen said to the council, "Ye stiffnecked and uncircumcised in heart and ears, ye do always resist the Holy Ghost" (Acts 7:51, KJV). The prophets warn that a heart repeatedly hardened can become unable to respond (Isaiah 6:9–10). Yet they also promise: "Come now, and let us reason together, saith the LORD: though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow" (Isaiah 1:18, KJV).`,
    theological: `## Answering the anxious
1. **Desire for forgiveness is the sign of the Spirit's work.** Those who have committed the sin in Jesus' sense do not care about being forgiven.
2. **Christ's promise:** "him that cometh to me I will in no wise cast out" (John 6:37, KJV).
3. **The gospel is sufficient:** "There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus" (Romans 8:1, KJV).
4. **Warning to the hardened.** Persistent rejection of the Spirit's witness is perilous. "To day if ye will hear his voice, harden not your hearts" (Hebrews 3:7–8, KJV).

## Interpretations
- **A specific sin of Jesus' time:** attributing Christ's works to Satan while He was on earth (some interpreters think it cannot be repeated in the same way).
- **A final unbelief:** persistent rejection of the gospel until death (Augustine, many Reformers).
- **Apostasy by those who knew the truth** (an Arminian reading of Hebrews 6 and 10).
All agree that it is not committed accidentally.`,
    views: `The traditions differ in detail but agree that: (1) the unforgivable sin is rare and extreme; (2) anyone who fears they have committed it, and is willing to repent, has not; and (3) pastors should reassure the anxious with the promises of the gospel.`,
    pastoral: `When someone says "I think I have committed the unforgivable sin", do not argue. Listen, show that their distress is evidence of the Spirit's work, read John 6:37 and 1 John 1:9 with them, and encourage them to confess and to trust Christ. Persistent obsessive anxiety may also need help from a doctor or counsellor; pray and refer with compassion.` },

  { slug: "does-god-change-his-mind", category: "God, evil and judgement",
    title: "Does God change His mind? What does 'God repented' mean?",
    summary: "The Bible says both that God does not change or repent (Numbers 23:19; Malachi 3:6) and that He 'repented' of planned judgement when people repented (Exodus 32:14; Jonah 3:10). The solution is that God's character, purposes and promises never change, while He responds in real relationship to people's actions, so that His warnings are conditional and His mercy is free. 'Repent' describes God's compassion, not regret over a mistake or a lack of knowledge.",
    scriptures: ["Numbers 23:19", "1 Samuel 15:11", "1 Samuel 15:29", "Exodus 32:7-14", "Jeremiah 18:5-10", "Jonah 3:1-10", "Jonah 4:1-2", "Malachi 3:6", "James 1:17", "Hebrews 6:17-18", "Genesis 6:5-8"],
    readings: ["Terence Fretheim, The Suffering of God", "Bruce Ware, God's Lesser Glory (open theism critique)", "John Frame, The Doctrine of God", "Clark Pinnock et al., The Openness of God (for the open-theist view)", "Wayne Grudem, Systematic Theology (God's immutability and prayer)"],
    exegetical: `## God does not change
"God is not a man, that he should lie; neither the son of man, that he should repent: hath he said, and shall he not do it? or hath he spoken, and shall he not make it good?" (Numbers 23:19, KJV). "For I am the LORD, I change not; therefore ye sons of Jacob are not consumed" (Malachi 3:6, KJV). James says God is the Father of lights, "with whom is no variableness, neither shadow of turning" (James 1:17, KJV). Hebrews says that God confirmed His promise by an oath so that "by two immutable things, in which it was impossible for God to lie" we might have strong consolation (Hebrews 6:18).

## God "repents"
- **Before the flood:** "And it repented the LORD that he had made man on the earth, and it grieved him at his heart" (Genesis 6:6, KJV).
- **Saul:** "It repenteth me that I have set up Saul to be king" (1 Samuel 15:11, KJV).
- **After the golden calf, Moses intercedes:** "And the LORD repented of the evil which he thought to do unto his people" (Exodus 32:14, KJV).
- **Nineveh:** "And God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil, that he had said that he would do unto them; and he did it not" (Jonah 3:10, KJV). Jonah knew this would happen: "I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil" (Jonah 4:2, KJV).

## The apparent contradiction, and the answer
1 Samuel 15 itself holds both: verse 11, "It repenteth me", and verse 29, "the Strength of Israel will not lie nor repent: for he is not a man, that he should repent." The same chapter uses the word in two senses.
- **In one sense** (1 Samuel 15:29, Numbers 23:19) *repent* means to change one's settled purpose or break a promise, which God never does.
- **In the other sense** (Genesis 6:6, Exodus 32:14, Jonah 3:10) the Hebrew *nacham* (H5162) means to feel sorrow or compassion, and to change course in response to a changed situation, as when a doctor changes treatment because the patient has changed.
Jeremiah 18:7–10 states the principle: God warns a nation of judgement, and if it turns from evil, "I will repent of the evil that I thought to do unto them". Warnings of judgement (such as Jonah's "Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown") carry an implied condition: they are meant to produce repentance, and when they do, God's unchanging mercy is released.`,
    archaeological: `Pagan gods in the ancient Near East were capricious and could be bribed or provoked (the Atrahasis flood story has the gods sending the flood because humans were too noisy). The God of the Bible is both unchanging in His character and responsive to repentance. In Nineveh, excavations of the library of Ashurbanipal show that the Assyrians practised rituals of mourning and fasting for averting divine anger, including royal fasts and sackcloth, in times of omens and eclipse, a cultural context in which the king's decree in Jonah 3:6–9 is intelligible. (The Assyrian Eponym Chronicle records a solar eclipse in 763 BC and plague in this period, but the text of Jonah does not mention them.)`,
    apostolic: `The apostles teach that God "worketh all things after the counsel of his own will" (Ephesians 1:11) and also urge prayer, trusting that He hears (1 John 5:14–15). Peter says that God is "not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance" (2 Peter 3:9, KJV), which explains why He delays judgement. The unchanging God invites the changing person.`,
    prophetic: `Joel 2:13–14 echoes Exodus 34:6: "rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil. Who knoweth if he will return and repent, and leave a blessing behind him?" (KJV). The prophets appeal to God's character of mercy to motivate repentance.`,
    theological: `## Classical teaching
Christian theology since the Fathers speaks of God's **immutability**: He is unchanging in His being, character, will and promises. The language of repentance is **anthropomorphic** (describing God in human terms so we can understand Him): Scripture is written so that humans can grasp how God relates to them. Calvin said God "lisps" to us as a nurse to a child.

## Relational response
Yet the Bible's picture is of a God who really relates: He hears prayer, is grieved by sin, rejoices over repentance. Moses' intercession in Exodus 32 is not pretend. Christians differ in how they explain this:
- **Classical theism:** God eternally knows and ordains the whole story, including the prayers and responses of His people; what looks like change is the working out of His eternal plan, in which prayer is a means He appoints.
- **Open theism:** the future is partly open and God responds in real time, so He can truly change His plans, though He is not mistaken (a minority view that most evangelical bodies reject because of Scripture's teaching on God's foreknowledge, Isaiah 46:9–10).
- **Middle positions:** God's character and ultimate purposes are fixed, while the way He accomplishes them is flexibly responsive.

## Practical meaning
1. **Trust His promises.** What He has sworn will stand.
2. **Pray with confidence.** He hears and acts in response.
3. **Repent quickly.** Warnings are mercies.
4. **Do not presume.** A delayed judgement is not a cancelled one if there is no repentance.`,
    views: `The classical, Reformed, Catholic and Orthodox traditions teach immutability with a real relational language; open theists (Pinnock, Sanders, Boyd) hold that God can change His mind in response to free choices; process theology denies God's immutability altogether, which most Christians reject.`,
    pastoral: `For those wrestling with prayer: God invites you to ask and to intercede like Moses. For those afraid He will change His mind about them: if you are in Christ, His love is settled, and "the gifts and calling of God are without repentance" (Romans 11:29, KJV).` },

  { slug: "can-a-christian-have-a-demon", category: "Church and ministry",
    title: "Can a Christian be demon-possessed? What about spiritual warfare?",
    summary: "The New Testament teaches that believers belong to Christ and are indwelt by the Holy Spirit, and it never describes a believer as 'possessed' by a demon. It does warn that believers can be tempted, deceived, oppressed, give the devil a foothold and need deliverance from sinful strongholds. Christians differ about the language ('possession', 'oppression', 'influence'), but agree on the authority of Christ, the weapons of Scripture, prayer and holiness, and the need for pastoral wisdom and care.",
    scriptures: ["Mark 1:21-28", "Luke 13:10-17", "Acts 5:1-11", "Acts 16:16-18", "Acts 19:11-20", "1 Corinthians 6:19-20", "2 Corinthians 10:3-5", "Ephesians 4:26-27", "Ephesians 6:10-18", "James 4:7", "1 Peter 5:8-9", "1 John 4:1-4"],
    readings: ["Clinton Arnold, Three Crucial Questions about Spiritual Warfare", "Sydney Page, Powers of Evil: A Biblical Study of Satan and Demons", "Michael Green, I Believe in Satan's Downfall", "Craig Keener, Miracles (volume 2, on exorcism)", "Wayne Grudem, Systematic Theology (chapter on angels, Satan and demons)", "Kenneth Kantzer and others, Demon Possession (Moody)"],
    exegetical: `## The word "possessed"
The English "demon-possessed" translates the Greek *daimonizomai* (G1139), "to be demonised" or "under the power of a demon". It appears in the Gospels and once in Acts. The New Testament never uses it of a believer who has the Holy Spirit.

## What Scripture says about believers
- "What? know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God, and ye are not your own?" (1 Corinthians 6:19, KJV).
- "Ye are of God, little children, and have overcome them: because greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world" (1 John 4:4, KJV).
- God "hath delivered us from the power of darkness, and hath translated us into the kingdom of his dear Son" (Colossians 1:13, KJV).
- "And what agreement hath the temple of God with idols?" (2 Corinthians 6:16, KJV).
Paul never commands the churches to cast demons out of believers; he commands them to resist, stand firm, put on the armour of God and put off sinful practices.

## Texts that raise the question
- **Luke 13:10–17.** The woman bent double for eighteen years had "a spirit of infirmity", and Jesus calls her "a daughter of Abraham, whom Satan hath bound". Some see a believer under demonic affliction; others note that she is not described as indwelt by the Spirit and that this is a physical illness linked to Satan.
- **Acts 5:3.** "Ananias, why hath Satan filled thine heart to lie to the Holy Ghost?" shows that a professing believer can be strongly influenced by Satan. It is not "possession" in the Gospels' sense.
- **Ephesians 4:27.** "Neither give place to the devil" (*topos*, G5117, "a place, a foothold"). Believers can give the devil opportunity by unresolved anger and sin.
- **1 Peter 5:8.** "Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour."

## The main positions
1. **Believers cannot be possessed** because the Holy Spirit lives in them, but they can be oppressed, tempted and deceived (most Reformed and evangelical writers, such as Grudem and Arnold).
2. **Believers can be inhabited by demons in part of their lives** (some deliverance ministries). They point to experience and to the case of Ananias, and say that the Spirit occupies the human spirit while a demon may affect the soul or body. This is not clearly taught in Scripture and is rejected by most scholars.
3. **A non-spatial view:** "demonisation" is a scale of influence, from temptation to control, and the question of "inside" or "outside" is not the right one.`,
    archaeological: `- **Exorcism in the ancient world.** Josephus (*Antiquities* 8.45–49) saw a Jewish exorcist using a ring and a root and the name of Solomon. The Greek Magical Papyri (Egypt, first to fifth centuries AD) preserve many spells and exorcism formulae, some using the name of Jesus or Jewish names of God. The sons of Sceva (Acts 19:13–16) used the name of Jesus as a formula and failed, and Ephesus was a city famous for magical books ("Ephesian letters"); the burning of the scrolls (Acts 19:19) matches this setting. The 50,000 pieces of silver involved (Acts 19:19) points to a substantial local trade.
- **Amulets and curse tablets** (Greek and Latin *defixiones*), found across the Roman world, including Corinth and Ephesus, show how widespread fear of spirits was. Christianity addressed it with the sufficiency of Christ.
- **Early church practice.** Justin Martyr (about AD 155) and Irenaeus (about AD 180) report that Christians cast out demons in Jesus' name without magical rites. The early church developed catechumen exorcisms before baptism as a renunciation of the devil and his works.`,
    apostolic: `The apostles' main teaching on spiritual warfare is in Ephesians 6:10–18: "Be strong in the Lord, and in the power of his might. Put on the whole armour of God, that ye may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil." The armour consists of truth, righteousness, the gospel of peace, faith, salvation, the word of God and prayer. James says, "Resist the devil, and he will flee from you" (James 4:7, KJV), and Paul writes that "the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty through God to the pulling down of strong holds" (2 Corinthians 10:4, KJV), which he applies to arguments and thoughts that oppose the knowledge of God.`,
    prophetic: `Jesus said that the signs following believers include "In my name shall they cast out devils" (Mark 16:17, KJV; a passage with a debated textual history). Jesus gave His disciples authority over demons (Luke 10:17–20) but said: "rejoice not, that the spirits are subject unto you; but rather rejoice, because your names are written in heaven." The final defeat of Satan is certain (Revelation 20:10).`,
    theological: `## Guidelines for balanced teaching
1. **Christ has won** (Colossians 2:15). Believers fight from victory, not for victory.
2. **Neither deny nor obsess.** C. S. Lewis warned that there are two equal and opposite errors: disbelieving in devils, or taking an excessive and unhealthy interest in them.
3. **Most Christian struggle is with the world, the flesh and the devil together** (Ephesians 2:2–3), and calls for repentance, truth, prayer, fellowship and discipline, not necessarily "deliverance".
4. **Do not blame the devil for everything.** Sin has its own sources: "every man is tempted, when he is drawn away of his own lust, and enticed" (James 1:14, KJV).
5. **Care for the mentally ill.** Epilepsy, psychosis, trauma and other illnesses can be mistaken for demonic activity; and sometimes both are present. Seek medical and pastoral help together.
6. **Practise deliverance prayer carefully:** in the name of Jesus, with at least two mature believers, in a respectful and non-sensational way, never harming or humiliating the person, and with accountability.`,
    views: `Pentecostal and charismatic churches generally emphasise deliverance ministry; Reformed and Baptist churches stress the believer's security in Christ and the sufficiency of Scripture and prayer. Some well-known ministries (Neil Anderson's "Freedom in Christ", Derek Prince, Peter Wagner) use different vocabulary and methods. African and Asian Christians often face spiritual realities more openly than Western Christians, and Western theologians have been learning from them.`,
    pastoral: `Meet people with calm and compassion. Pray, read Scripture and offer repentance and forgiveness where they are needed. Avoid shouting, theatrics or fear-based claims. If symptoms continue, involve doctors and mature leaders. Remind the congregation that a Christian's greatest protection is a life of faith, honesty, fellowship and obedience.` },
];
